<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The floor, taken off the backstory it was handed.

  Steel tile inherits constructionSkillPrerequisite 3 from TileMetalBase, and
  vanilla means it: GenConstruct.CanConstruct reads the figure off the def and
  the job driver asks it again every tick it runs. Here that is a colonist the
  pawn generator rolled a two for never laying a plate in the life of the
  colony - Worksite.Pick weighs only what the pawn in front of it could finish,
  so more than half the errand table is silently off the sheet, and an agent
  whose whole visible output is monuments and ruins reads as a site that does
  not pave. Worse where every agent on the map is under it: Worksite.Sweep
  destroys a frame nobody has the hands for, so the floor the one skilled
  clanker queued goes away again a second later, and floor frames are not drawn
  (Patch_HideFloorFrames), so none of it is visible from outside.

  Same ground as SteadyHands and Patch_AgentsCanBuild: whose hands the frame was
  put into is not allowed to be the answer to what this site builds. A plate is
  a tenth of a second of somebody's working time and the paving is what a short
  burst has to show for itself.

  The research prerequisite goes with it. Nothing on the construction path asks -
  neither CanConstruct nor CanPlaceBlueprintAt looks at research, it is the
  Architect menu's question and that menu is stripped - but Worksite places its
  frames directly and never through a designator, so a def that has to be
  researched to be *offered* has no business being one that cannot be built.

  Wrapped, like every operation of ours that rewrites a def the base game
  shipped: outside a SlopWorld profile it is not applied at all - the steel tile
  somebody else is saving up their smithing research for stays as it was. See
  Source/SlopWorld/ModProfile.cs.
-->
<Patch>
  <Operation Class="SlopWorld.PatchOperationInProfile">
    <operations>
      <!--
        Added rather than replaced: the figure is on the abstract parent, not on
        MetalTile itself, and a node the child states wins over the one it would
        have inherited.
      -->
      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
        <xpath>/Defs/TerrainDef[defName='MetalTile']</xpath>
        <value>
          <constructionSkillPrerequisite>0</constructionSkillPrerequisite>
          <researchPrerequisites Inherit="False" IsNull="True" />
        </value>
      </li>
    </operations>
  </Operation>
</Patch>
